diffh -- Command

The half-hearted differencing algorithm, run by diff -h and diff -d
/usr/lib/diffh [-bdefhs] [-c symbol] file1 file2

diffh is a second implementation of diff(1) that trades minimal output for
bounded memory.  It is not meant to be typed: diff execs it, with the command
line diff itself was given, in two cases.

    diff -h		always
    diff -d		when either file is larger than 25000 bytes

diff proper reads both files into memory and finds the shortest edit script by
the Hirschberg algorithm, so its answer is minimal but its appetite grows with
the files.  diffh streams both files instead, holding at most a few hundred
lines of either at a time, and resynchronises the moment it sees two lines in
a row that match.  It therefore runs on files of any size in a fixed amount of
memory, and reports differences that are correct but not always the smallest
ones there are.

That trade is worth making where changes are small and well separated, which
is what -h is for, and it is the only way to compare two files that will not
both fit, which is what -d is for.

Since diff hands its whole command line over unchanged, diffh accepts every
diff option and reports differences in the same formats: the default ed-style
listing, -e and -f scripts, -c for a C preprocessor merge, -s for a summary
count and -b to ignore trailing and repeated white space.  Its exit status is
diff's: 0 if the files are the same, 1 if they differ and 2 on error.

Files
/usr/lib/diffh	this program
/bin/diff	the caller

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it.  The Lexicon's diff
article names /usr/lib/diffh as the program -h and -d run, but does not
describe it, and this system installs it as a program of its own.  The page
was written here, from the source in base/cmd/diff; none of Mark Williams
Company's text is in it.

diffh and diff share the file comparison, output and option-parsing code and
differ only in the algorithm that finds the differences, so anything true of
diff's output is true of this one's.

The 25000-byte threshold at which -d switches over is compiled in.

